    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Functional hail damage affects a roof's ability to keep water out or shortens how long it will last. Cosmetic damage changes only how the roof looks. The distinction matters only if your policy has a cosmetic damage exclusion; without one, a hail-dented roof is direct physical damage like any other. With one, dents alone usually aren't covered, and the claim depends on proving that the hail did more than mar the surface.</p>
<p>These exclusions are common on metal roofs in Texas, and some insurers attach them to other roof types too. Many homeowners don't know they have one until the denial letter quotes it.</p>

<h2>Do you have a cosmetic damage exclusion?</h2>
<p>Look at the list of forms and endorsements on your declarations page. The exclusion is usually a separate endorsement with a title along the lines of "Cosmetic Damage Exclusion," "Roof Surface Cosmetic Loss Exclusion," or "Limitations on Roof Coverage." It may have been added at a renewal, often with a notice that was easy to overlook. Request a complete copy of the policy in effect on your storm date if the endorsement isn't in your paperwork.</p>
<p>If there's no such endorsement, the insurer may still argue that dents aren't "damage," but it has a much weaker position. Policies generally cover direct physical loss, and a dented panel has been physically changed.</p>

<h2>How to read the endorsement</h2>
<p>Cosmetic exclusions aren't all written the same way, and small differences decide claims. Typical language excludes marring, pitting, denting or other damage that changes the roof's appearance but doesn't keep it from working as a barrier against the weather the way it did before. Read yours with these questions in mind.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Which surfaces does it cover?</strong> Some exclusions apply only to metal roofing. Others apply to all roof coverings, or to roof surfaces plus siding, gutters and other exterior metal. If yours names only the roof, dented gutters, downspouts and window wraps may still be covered.</li>
<li><strong>Which roof types?</strong> An exclusion limited to "metal roof surfacing" doesn't reach asphalt shingles on another part of the house.</li>
<li><strong>How does it define function?</strong> This is the most important question. Some endorsements refer only to the roof's ability to keep water out. Others mention its expected life or ability to protect the structure. If the wording mentions only water entry, an argument that hail shortened the roof's service life may not work. If it mentions life expectancy, it may.</li>
<li><strong>Does it say "to the same extent as before"?</strong> That phrase compares the roof's performance before and after the storm, which opens the door to evidence about how dents affect drainage, coatings and fasteners over time.</li>
<li><strong>Is there an exception for openings?</strong> Many exclusions expressly don't apply when hail creates an opening, puncture or crack that lets water in.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Copy the exact sentence of the exclusion into your notes and quote it in every letter. Disputes tend to drift toward "the roof isn't really damaged" when the real question is what that specific sentence covers.</p></div>

<h2>What functional damage looks like in practice</h2>
<p>Functional damage is anything that reduces the roof's water-shedding ability or its service life. Here's how that plays out on common Texas roofs.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Usually argued as functional</h3><ul><li>Punctures, splits or tears in any roof covering</li><li>Cracked paint or coating at the center of metal dents, exposing bare metal that will corrode</li><li>Dented standing seams that no longer lock tightly</li><li>Fastener heads or washers struck and cracked on exposed-fastener panels</li><li>Dents on low-slope metal deep enough to hold water</li><li>Fractured shingle mats and bruises on asphalt</li><li>Cracked tile, punctured flat-roof membranes, crushed insulation</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>Usually argued as cosmetic</h3><ul><li>Shallow dents in metal panels with paint intact</li><li>Dents in thick-gauge trim that sheds water normally</li><li>Spatter marks and surface scuffs</li><li>Minor granule displacement without mat damage</li><li>Dents in gutters that still drain properly (under exclusions that cover gutters)</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>The line isn't fixed. A dent with intact paint may develop cracks as the panel heats and cools. Coating damage that's invisible from the ground can show up under magnification. That's why documentation from roof level, not from a distance, decides these claims. Our <a href="/post/how-to-identify-hail-damage-by-roof-type/">guide to hail damage by roof type</a> covers what to look for on each material.</p>

<h2>Metal roofs: where most disputes happen</h2>
<p>Metal roofs are where cosmetic exclusions are most common and where the arguments are most technical. Expect the insurer's position to be that dents don't leak. Expect yours to be that hail did more than dent.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Coatings.</strong> Metal roofing relies on its galvanized or Galvalume layer and paint system to resist corrosion. Hail that cracks the coating at an impact point exposes the steel. Close-up and magnified photos of crazing at dent centers are some of the strongest evidence you can gather.</li>
<li><strong>Seams.</strong> On standing seam roofs, dents on the seam can open the lock or distort the panel enough to affect how it moves with temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Fasteners.</strong> On R-panel and other exposed-fastener roofs, a hailstone that hits a screw head can crack the washer, and a cracked washer is a leak point.</li>
<li><strong>Drainage.</strong> On low-slope metal, closely spaced dents can trap water. Standing water on metal speeds corrosion.</li>
</ul>
<p>Trim, ridge caps and flashings on metal roofs are often thinner than the panels and may show punctures or splits when the panels don't. Check them separately.</p>

<h2>Shingle roofs and cosmetic exclusions</h2>
<p>Some exclusions also apply to asphalt shingle roofs. In practice, true hail damage to shingles is rarely purely cosmetic: a bruise that fractures the mat, or a hit that removes granules down to bare asphalt, affects how long the shingle will last. Insurers sometimes call minor granule loss "cosmetic," which is where clear roof-level photos of bruises and exposed mat matter. Separately, the insurer may say granule loss is age-related wear; that's a different argument, covered in our article on <a href="/post/how-insurance-companies-evaluate-hail-damage/">how insurance companies evaluate hail damage</a>.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>How cosmetic disputes play out</h2>
<p>Most cosmetic-exclusion disputes follow a similar path.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>The denial or partial denial.</strong> The insurer's adjuster confirms dents, cites the exclusion, and pays for items it considers functional, such as a punctured vent or a broken skylight, if anything.</li>
<li><strong>Your evidence.</strong> You or your public adjuster document coating cracks, seam damage, fastener damage and punctures from roof level, slope by slope, and quote the endorsement's definition of function.</li>
<li><strong>Re-inspection or engineer.</strong> The insurer often sends an engineer to examine the roof. Engineers' reports tend to focus on whether the roof currently leaks. Be there, point out specific impacts, and ask that every slope be examined.</li>
<li><strong>Decision.</strong> The insurer revises, or holds its position. If it holds, the dispute is about coverage, not price.</li>
</ol>
<p>That last point matters. The appraisal clause in most policies is for disagreements about the <em>amount</em> of loss, not about whether a loss is covered. If the insurer agrees the panels are functionally damaged but disputes the cost, appraisal may help; see <a href="/post/appraisal-for-hail-damage-claim/">appraisal for hail damage claims</a>. If the insurer's position is that the damage falls within the exclusion, appraisal may not resolve it, and the remaining options are a complaint to the Texas Department of Insurance or a review by an attorney. Our guide to a <a href="/post/denied-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">denied hail damage claim</a> covers those steps.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Watch for partial roofs:</strong> an insurer may accept functional damage on a few panels and offer to replace only those. If replacement panels won't match or can't be installed without disturbing the rest of the roof, that becomes a repair-scope and matching question. Read <a href="/post/matching-laws-for-roof-replacement-in-texas/">matching and roof replacement in Texas</a>.</p></div>

<h2>Before you buy or renew: check your roof coverage</h2>
<p>If you're replacing a roof after a storm, ask your agent whether your policy will carry a cosmetic exclusion afterward and whether it applies to the new material. Some homeowners choose impact-resistant shingles or metal for durability and later find their coverage for hail on that roof has narrowed. Get the answer in writing, and compare it with other policies at renewal if hail coverage matters to you.</p>
<p>For roof claims where a cosmetic exclusion is in play, we start by reading the endorsement before we inspect, so the inspection targets the damage that wording covers. See our <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail damage claims</a> service for how we document metal and shingle roofs.</p>
